{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794173001,"nonce":"iCzcMTBY8CkSPn0wDiiiddZ54tg=","title":"jira sync --dry-run plans against the schema the run would derive","message":"Found in a real Jira sync: Jira added an existing status to an issue type's workflow. The dry run printed the derived schema change (the new status value), then reported 44 issues pending with `Jira status \"\u003cname\u003e\" (\u003cid\u003e) is not a value of status`. The real run imported them all and left nothing pending.\n\nThe dry run derives the change but leaves it unapplied, then checks the issues against the stored schema. The real run checks them against the schema it has just written.\n\n## Design\n\n- The dry run applies the derived schema in memory, writes nothing, and checks every issue against that schema, exactly as the real run checks against the schema it writes. Its plan is the real run's plan.\n- `doc/design/jira.md` states the dry run's contract wherever it describes the dry run.\n\n## Test\n\n- Add a status to the fake Jira's workflow and give an issue that status: the dry run reports the issue imported, not pending, and the store's schema is unchanged.","files":null,"fields":{"area":["bridge"],"parent":"8ade81134de7c2f5d1a16c3a48efc761b35bbf4cc2a58eaf8189fa5c15621e01","priority":"high","status":"to-do","type":"task"}}]}